    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe behaviour-level redesign is placed roughly where it belongs (Soundex extracted to its own model class, identity logic kept inside `OwnerIdentity`, soft-match kept inside `CheckPossibleDuplicate`), but the change introduces duplication and leaves sibling classes describing a world that no longer exists.\n\n**1. `sha256hex` is now copy-pasted verbatim into `OwnerIdentity`.**\n`OwnerIdentity.sha256hex` (model/OwnerIdentity.java:45-60) is byte-for-byte identical to `OwnerHousehold.sha256hex` (rest/function/owner/OwnerHousehold.java:58-73) \u2014 same body, same javadoc, same `NoSuchAlgorithmException` handling. Before this change `OwnerIdentity` built its key by string concatenation and had no hashing; the diff copied the helper rather than sharing it. Extract one shared helper (e.g. a small `Sha256`/hex util next to `OwnerIdentity` in the model package) and call it from both. As written, the two hex-encoding routines can silently drift.\n\n**2. `OwnerHousehold`'s \"single definition of same household\" doc is now false.**\nIts class javadoc (OwnerHousehold.java:22-25) still names `CheckPossibleDuplicate` among the steps that key off `householdId`, and claims changing the keying rule \"happens here, once.\" But `CheckPossibleDuplicate` no longer touches `OwnerHousehold`/`householdId` at all \u2014 it matches on `soundex(lastName) + postcode` directly. A maintainer editing `OwnerHousehold` to change household matching would not realize the soft-match path is now unaffected. Update the doc to reflect that only `CountHouseholdMembers`/`CapMembershipLevel` still key off it.\n\n**3. `AssignHousehold`'s doc still claims `householdId` feeds the identity key.**\nAssignHousehold.java:18-20 states \"The `householdId` is a component of the owner's `OwnerIdentity` key and is the single value that duplicate detection \u2026 key off.\" After this change `OwnerIdentity` no longer uses `householdId`, and duplicate detection (hard and soft) no longer keys off it. This is now actively misleading and should be corrected to scope `householdId` to the household-size count.\n\n**4. Postcode normalization re-inlined, bypassing the shared household key.**\n`CheckPossibleDuplicate.normalizePostcode` (`postcode.strip()`, lines 65-67) re-implements the exact postcode normalization `OwnerHousehold` centralizes (`postcode.strip()` in its compact constructor). The soft-match is conceptually a household-similarity key (\"surname sound + postcode\"), yet it's computed inline here instead of through the abstraction that exists precisely to hold that rule \"once.\" Either express the soundex+postcode match through a shared helper (mirroring `OwnerHousehold`) or, at minimum, don't duplicate the postcode-normalization rule in a second place where it can diverge from `OwnerHousehold`.\n\nFixing #1 (shared hash helper) and reconciling the stale docs in #2/#3 are the most important; #4 is the judgment call about keeping household keying in one unit.",
